1
00:00:04,090 --> 00:00:08,930
Long after the rockets disappeared into the sky, something remained.

2
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:12,520
Across the silent landscapes of the Moon and Mars,

3
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:17,670
machines, instruments, and fragments of human exploration still stand.

4
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:23,027
They are reminders of journeys that changed our understanding of the universe.

5
00:00:24,627 --> 00:00:29,617
In 1969, Apollo 11 brought humanity to the Moon.

6
00:00:29,837 --> 00:00:34,567
But when the astronauts returned home, they did not bring everything back.

7
00:00:35,297 --> 00:00:38,907
The descent stages of lunar modules, scientific instruments,

8
00:00:38,937 --> 00:00:42,257
cameras, and other equipment were left behind.

9
00:00:42,937 --> 00:00:45,037
Each object had a purpose.

10
00:00:45,067 --> 00:00:48,877
Each became part of the history of lunar exploration.

11
00:00:50,581 --> 00:00:53,981
Later Apollo missions left even more behind.

12
00:00:54,341 --> 00:00:58,771
The Lunar Roving Vehicle carried astronauts across the lunar surface.

13
00:00:58,801 --> 00:01:01,901
Scientific experiment packages studied the Moon.

14
00:01:01,931 --> 00:01:07,121
Retroreflectors still help scientists measure the distance between Earth and the Moon.

15
00:01:07,721 --> 00:01:11,940
Some of these forgotten machines continue to serve science today.

16
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:15,400
Then came Mars.

17
00:01:15,670 --> 00:01:19,840
In 1976, the Viking landers reached the Red Planet.

18
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:24,500
Pathfinder and its tiny companion, Sojourner, followed decades later.

19
00:01:24,530 --> 00:01:31,010
Spirit, Opportunity, Curiosity, and Perseverance carried our search for knowledge even further.

20
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:36,510
These robotic explorers transformed distant landscapes into places we could study.

21
00:01:38,014 --> 00:01:41,094
But why were these machines left behind?

22
00:01:41,384 --> 00:01:44,644
Some were designed to remain after their missions ended.

23
00:01:44,674 --> 00:01:47,724
Others stopped working because of damaged systems,

24
00:01:47,754 --> 00:01:52,274
harsh environments, depleted power, or lost communication.

25
00:01:53,014 --> 00:01:59,814
The Moon and Mars preserve the physical traces of both successful missions and unexpected endings.

26
00:02:01,622 --> 00:02:05,712
From lunar landing stages to silent Martian rovers,

27
00:02:05,742 --> 00:02:08,602
every piece of hardware tells a story.

28
00:02:08,632 --> 00:02:11,232
Some still provide scientific value.

29
00:02:11,262 --> 00:02:16,292
Others survive through photographs, orbital observations, and mission records.

30
00:02:17,262 --> 00:02:24,772
What once represented the cutting edge of engineering has become a historical footprint of humanity beyond Earth.

31
00:02:26,395 --> 00:02:29,785
New missions will continue exploring these worlds.

32
00:02:29,985 --> 00:02:33,905
New machines will arrive, and new discoveries will be made.

33
00:02:34,485 --> 00:02:38,875
But the explorers who came before will never truly disappear.

34
00:02:38,905 --> 00:02:40,735
Their missions may have ended.

35
00:02:40,995 --> 00:02:43,075
Their legacy remains.
